    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d155">

                <pb facs="0218" n="136" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg136"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.34537/54.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the American Minister.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-04-12"><hi rendition="#italic">April 12,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 277.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s instruction
                    No. 278,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d155-f1"/> of this date, which acknowledges the
                    receipt of your dispatch No. 700 of March 25, 1911,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d155-f2"/> you are informed that the attitude of this Government
                    with regard to the final settlement of the sovereignty over the Isle of Pines
                    has been made clear in the treaty signed at Washington on March. 2, 1904, which
                    has been submitted to the Senate for its approval but on which no action has as
                    yet been taken. The Department therefore considers that the wisest course is to
                    allow the matter to remain where it at present rests, and you will so inform the
                    Cuban secretary of state.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d155-f1" n="1" xml:id="d155-f1-content">
                <p>The omissions appear under Naval Station at Guantánamo and Claim for the cost of
                    intervention in Cuba, <ref target="#pg118">pp. 118</ref> and <ref
                        target="#pg134">134</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d155-f2" n="3" xml:id="d155-f2-content">
